<section class="container" data-reveal-root>
  <header class="page-head" data-reveal>
    <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>Live demo · the agent, running</p>
    <h1>Live Demo</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is not a recording. It is the agent itself — the same Phaser game and the
      same DQN from this repository — playing, dying, and learning in your browser.
      Leave it open and it keeps training; close it and IndexedDB remembers.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div data-reveal>
    {% include "components/demo-panel.njk" %}
  </div>

  <div class="demo-notes" data-reveal>
    <article class="demo-note">
      <h3>Nobody is playing</h3>
      <p>
        There is no human input and no scripted behavior. Every flap is the output of
        the network — or, while ε is high, of the exploration sampler.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="demo-note">
      <h3>Progress persists locally</h3>
      <p>
        Weights, replay memory, ε, and generation are saved to your browser's
        IndexedDB on every death. Your tab trains its own private lineage of the agent.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="demo-note">
      <h3>Read the HUD</h3>
      <p>
        The in-game overlay shows generation, ε, the raw state values, and both
        Q-values — Q-Idle and Q-Flap. The gap between them is the agent's confidence.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" data-reveal style="margin-top: 2rem;">
    <h2>Running it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      The demo is deployed straight from the repository. To run the training
      environment locally — or to reset the brain and start from generation 1 —
      clone the repo, install dependencies, and pick an experiment:
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/danrosselli/dqn-flappy-bird.git
cd dqn-flappy-bird
bun install
cd experiments/001-base
bun run dev</code></pre>
    <p>
      The game dev server starts on <code>localhost:8080</code>. Switch to any
      experiment directory (<code>002-state9</code>, etc.) to try a different
      configuration. Everything the agent learns stays in your browser; nothing
      leaves your machine.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
